        "Welcome to Day 46 of Advanced Data Structures and Algorithmic Optimization. Today we inaugurate Month 2 Block 10, dedicated to one of the most intellectually exhilarating and industrially vital domains in computer science: Advanced Network Flow Theory, Maximum Flow, and Minimum Cut Duality. Network flow problems model how conserved physical quantities—such as data packets across internet backbones, crude oil through pipelines, electrical current across grids, supply chain cargo across shipping lanes, or financial liquidity through clearinghouses—move from production sources to consumption sinks through capacity-constrained conduits.",
        "Formally, a Flow Network is defined as a directed graph G = (V, E) where each directed edge (u, v) in E possesses a non-negative real-valued capacity C(u, v) >= 0. If an edge does not exist between two vertices, we define its capacity as C(u, v) = 0. The network contains two distinguished vertices: a Source vertex s (from which flow originates and which has no incoming flow) and a Sink vertex t (where flow terminates and which has no outgoing flow). A valid flow f in G is a real-valued function f: V x V -> R that strictly satisfies three foundational mathematical invariants across the entire network.",
        "The first invariant is the Capacity Constraint: for every pair of vertices u and v, the flow assigned to the edge cannot exceed its physical conduit capacity: 0 <= f(u, v) <= C(u, v). Conduits cannot be overfilled. The second invariant is Skew Symmetry: for all vertices u and v, f(u, v) = -f(v, u). While physical fluids flow in one direction, skew symmetry provides mathematical elegance by treating reverse flow as negative flow, simplifying algebraic formulations. The third and most critical invariant is Flow Conservation: for every intermediate vertex u in V \\ {s, t}, the total flow entering u must precisely equal the total flow exiting u: sum_{v in V} f(v, u) = sum_{w in V} f(u, w). Intermediate vertices cannot store, generate, or destroy flow; they act purely as conservation routing junctions.",
